BODY = '''<h2>What OrthoInfo and the CDC Say</h2>
<p>The American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons runs OrthoInfo, and its page on crutches, canes and walkers gives a simple fitting rule. When you stand up straight, the top of the walker should reach the crease in your wrist, and your elbows should be slightly bent when you hold the handgrips. That page covers walkers in general and does not name rollators, but the same check works for a rollator's handles.</p>
<p>It also gives rules that apply to anything with handles. Never climb stairs or use an escalator with a walker. Do not pull on or tilt the walker to help you stand up. To sit, back up until your legs touch the chair, feel for the seat and lower yourself slowly, and to stand, push up with your arms. Check the rubber tips regularly and replace them if they become uneven or worn.</p>
<p>The CDC says more than 1 in 4 older adults falls each year, that fewer than half tell their doctor, and that a single fall doubles the chance of another.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>Lock the brakes before you sit.</strong> Drive says its brake is pushed down to lock the rollator in place. Do that before you sit or stand, and check that all four wheels are on even ground, as OrthoInfo advises for a walker.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Rollator</h2>
<h3>Handle height</h3>
<p>Stand up straight in the shoes you wear and have someone check that the handgrips sit at your wrist crease, with your elbows slightly bent. The handle ranges here run from 30 to 34 inches on the steel Drive up to 33 to 40 inches on the HOMLAND.</p>
<h3>Weight limit</h3>
<p>The limits are 300 pounds on the aluminum Drive, 350 on the steel Drive and the Medline, and a recommended 350 on the VOCIC. The HOMLAND listing text gives none, so find its limit before you buy.</p>
<h3>Wheel size</h3>
<p>The wheels are 6 inches on the Medline and the aluminum Drive, 7.5 on the steel Drive and 8 on the VOCIC and the HOMLAND. Larger wheels roll over thresholds and uneven paths more easily. Smaller ones suit smooth floors.</p>
<h3>Weight and folding</h3>
<p>You will lift it into a car, so the weight counts. The aluminum Drive is 13 pounds on Drive's page, the Medline 16.5, the steel Drive 18.5, the HOMLAND 20.4 and the VOCIC about 22.</p>
<h3>Seat</h3>
<p>The seat is for a short rest, not for riding. The seats run from 12 by 12 inches on the Drive steel and the Medline to 16 by 12 on the HOMLAND.</p>

<h2>Rollator Questions</h2>
<h3>What is the difference between a rollator and a walker?</h3>
<p>A standard walker has four legs and you lift it with each step. A rollator has wheels and hand brakes, so you push it, and it has a seat. OrthoInfo's page describes the lifting kind, and its fit rule applies to both.</p>
<h3>Can I use a rollator on stairs?</h3>
<p>No. OrthoInfo says never to climb stairs or use an escalator with a walker, and a rollator is a walker with wheels. Use the handrail with one hand and a cane or a helper's arm with the other, or ask someone to carry the rollator and stay with you. Keep a second one upstairs if you need one on both floors.</p>
<h3>How do I know the handles are the right height?</h3>
<p>Stand up straight and let your arms hang. The top of the handgrip should reach the crease in your wrist, and your elbows should bend slightly when you hold it, as OrthoInfo says. Ask a physical therapist to check it if it feels wrong.</p>
<h3>Is it safe to sit on a rollator?</h3>
<p>The seat is made for a short rest, not for riding. Lock the brakes first, keep all four wheels on even ground, and lower yourself slowly, as OrthoInfo advises for sitting with a walker. Check that the seat is rated for your weight, since the seat's limit can differ from the frame's, and do not let anyone push you while you sit.</p>
<h3>How much does a rollator weigh?</h3>
<p>Drive's page lists 13 pounds for its aluminum rollator and 18.5 for the steel one with 7.5-inch wheels. The listings give 16.5 pounds for the Medline, 20.4 for the HOMLAND and about 22 for the VOCIC. If you lift it into a car, try the weight first, and remember that the frame without the seat or bag may weigh less than the figure in the listing.</p>
<h3>How do rollator brakes work?</h3>
<p>Drive says its brake is pressed to slow the rollator and pushed down to lock it in place. The Medline listing says to push the handles up to brake and down to lock, and the HOMLAND listing describes loop brakes with three modes: free, deceleration and stop. Lock the brakes every time you sit or stand, and check them before you go out.</p>

<h2>What the Research Says</h2>
<p>The Cochrane review of exercise for preventing falls found balance and functional exercise cut the rate of falls by 24%, and it did not test walking aids. A rollator supports walking, and a physical therapist can add exercises that work on balance. See <a href="/articles/what-exercise-prevents-falls-cochrane-review/">what the review found</a>.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>The Drive Medical Rollator with 7.5-inch wheels is the best pick for most people. It is rated for 350 pounds, Drive's page gives its seat height, handle range and weight, and 51,609 reviews average 4.6 stars. It suits an average-height user, and its 12-inch seat is small.</p>
<p>If weight is the problem, the Drive Aluminum is about 5 pounds lighter at 13 pounds, with taller handles up to 37 inches, but the limit drops to 300 pounds. The Medline is the steel option with a back, and the sources for it are the Amazon listing alone.</p>
<p>For rough ground, the VOCIC has 8-inch wheels but weighs about 22 pounds. For a taller user, the HOMLAND has the widest seat and a handle range to 40 inches, but find its weight limit first.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The Drive Medical Rollator with 7.5-inch wheels is the best all-round rollator. Choose the Drive Aluminum for a light frame, the VOCIC for rough ground and the HOMLAND for a wide seat and tall handles. Fit the handles to your wrist crease as OrthoInfo says, lock the brakes before you sit and never take it on stairs. See also our guides to <a href="/best-shower-chairs/">shower chairs</a> and <a href="/best-bedside-commodes/">bedside commodes</a>.</p>'''

